<section class="reference-section" id="varaha-earth"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Varaha · The Rescue of the Earth</h2><a href="/varaha-earth.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition, first volume.</strong> Original English retelling based on Skandha 3, chapter 13 and chapters 17–19 of the supplied edition. The book scan is not distributed by this website.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Open the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>3.13.6–17</strong><small>Manu’s charge, the submerged Earth and Brahma’s appeal.</small></li><li><strong>3.13.18–32</strong><small>The small boar’s manifestation, growth, descent, rescue and summary of the confrontation.</small></li><li><strong>3.13.33–45</strong><small>The sages’ praise, sacrificial symbolism and Earth’s restoration.</small></li><li><strong>3.17.16–31</strong><small>Hiranyaksha’s strength, search for battle and encounter with Varuna.</small></li><li><strong>3.18.1–8</strong><small>Narada’s direction, the challenge and the Earth made secure.</small></li><li><strong>3.18.9–28; 3.19.1–16</strong><small>The mace duel, Brahma’s appeal, the fallen mace, Sudarshana and the broken trident.</small></li><li><strong>3.19.17–31</strong><small>Illusions, their destruction, Hiranyaksha’s defeat, praise and the Lord’s return.</small></li><li><strong>3.19.32–38</strong><small>The telling’s conclusion and Vidura’s joy; the concluding blessings are not presented as modern guarantees.</small></li></ol><p>Chapter 13 summarizes events that chapters 17–19 expand. The longer Jaya–Vijaya background in chapters 15–16 is not retold here; 3.19.29 recalls the attendants’ eventual return. The rescue precedes the expanded duel in 3.18.8. The narrative does not add a claim that Hiranyaksha originally pushed the Earth into the waters.</p><p>Two reader paintings and a separate collection-card image are owner-supplied devotional artworks. Their anthropomorphic Varaha forms and globe-shaped Earth are artistic choices. Battle description is condensed and non-graphic. “Power that carries before it conquers” is an editorial reflection.</p></div></section>
